                    coach_r1_data:          `<strong>That's auditing with data, not opinions.</strong> Uptime 99.2% against a 99.9% SLA, 2.5% churn and 95% NRR put you firmly in the red. The product is not ready to absorb 3x volume and new markets — naming that honestly is the start of a real plan.`,
                    coach_r1_green:         `<strong>'These numbers are fine' is wishful thinking.</strong> 2.5% monthly churn and sub-100% NRR mean you are leaking customers every month. Calling that green is exactly how teams scale distribution on a product that can't hold the new load.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the metrics is taking the investor's side, not auditing.</strong> The market opportunity is real, but 'ready' is a threshold, not a feeling. Scale on a 99.2%-uptime platform and the opportunity becomes churn and reputational damage.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp audit.</strong> You named the product as not-ready AND located the highest-risk bottleneck in customer concentration — 55% of ARR in 3 accounts. That is the lens that stops you building expansion on a base that one non-renewal could collapse.`,
                    coach_r1_sales:         `<strong>'Under-investing in sales' is the investor's framing.</strong> With 2.5% churn and a 14-month CAC payback, more sales spend fills a leaky bucket faster. The bottleneck is the product and the concentration, not the sales budget.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>'Just execute faster' is how you multiply the churn.</strong> There absolutely is a bottleneck — a fragile product and 55% of ARR in three accounts. Faster execution on that base accelerates the damage, not the growth.`,
                    coach_r1_lowdata:       `<strong>This is taking a side, not modelling.</strong> Low data discipline means you'll decide by who argues hardest in the room. Ground it in uptime, churn, runway and concentration, or you'll relitigate the same fight every board meeting.`,
                    coach_r2_consolidate:   `<strong>Consolidate-and-scale buys you the runway to fix the product.</strong> €220k/mo burn gives ~24 months and funds the €600k re-platform. You enter France in month 9 from strength, not on a platform that breaks under new languages and 3x load.`,
                    coach_r2_hybrid:        `<strong>A soft hybrid feels safe but commits to nothing.</strong> Some product work and a vague pilot with no milestone gates leaves you neither stabilised nor fast — and the next board meeting reopens the same debate.`,
                    coach_r2_aggressive:    `<strong>You're scaling distribution ahead of the product.</strong> Selling in three countries on a 99.2%-uptime, 2.5%-churn platform with €450k/mo burn leaves 12 months of cash. If churn rises to 4% as you stress the product, how much NET ARR will you really have gained when the money runs out?`,
                    coach_r2_noproduct:     `<strong>You under-funded the product.</strong> The CTO needs ~€600k and 6–9 months to make the platform hold 3x volume and new markets. Skimp here and every new customer lands on the same fragile system that's already churning at 2.5%.`,
                    coach_r2_overspend:     `<strong>You tripled sales spend with churn still high.</strong> CAC payback is 14 months and churn 2.5% — you're filling a leaky bucket faster. Which metric has to move before you hit the sales accelerator? The churn, not the budget.`,
                    coach_r2_irreversible:  `<strong>You're under-weighting irreversible risk.</strong> Running out of cash, or burning your reputation with new-market customers on a product that fails, is hard to reverse. Ceding some speed is not. Reversibility should drive the order of your bets.`,
                    coach_r3_incentives:    `<strong>That's the structural read.</strong> It isn't that one side is right — the fund needs 3–5x in 5–7 years and the founder answers for survival and the product at 10 years. Both want to win; they don't want the same thing in the same timeframe. Design a plan that serves both horizons.`,
                    coach_r3_founder:       `<strong>Siding with the founder ignores the agency problem.</strong> The investor's pressure isn't ignorance — it's a fund horizon and a closing market window. Dismiss it and you lose the board's alignment and, with it, the next round.`,
                    coach_r3_investor:      `<strong>'Whoever pays decides' wins the meeting and loses the company.</strong> Overriding the founder and the CTO on product readiness is exactly how you scale onto a platform that can't hold it. Authority doesn't fix uptime or churn.`,
                    coach_r3_personal:      `<strong>This isn't a personality clash.</strong> Treating it as goodwill to be smoothed over misses that the conflict is structural — incentives and horizons. Name the incentives explicitly or you'll have the same argument next month.`,
                    coach_r3_aligned:       `<strong>Aligned and governed.</strong> You framed the disagreement as incentives and horizons AND proposed milestone-based funding both can sign — growth unlocked by product and customer metrics. That's how you serve a 5-year fund and a 10-year product at once.`,
                    coach_r3_trust:         `<strong>'We'll revisit it if something goes wrong' is not governance.</strong> By the time it visibly goes wrong, the cash is gone and the trust with it. Conditioned funding turns goodwill into a mechanism that holds under pressure.`,
                    coach_r3_veto:          `<strong>A spending veto hands the founder's fate to one horizon.</strong> It hard-wires the fund's incentive over the product's. The mechanism you want gates BOTH sides on shared metrics, not one side's control over the other.`,
                    coach_r3_concentration: `<strong>You skipped the concentration risk.</strong> Before opening three countries: if the largest client (~25% of ARR) doesn't renew, can you still finance the expansion? Was the round raised to grow, or to paper over a concentration risk?`,
                    coach_r4_strict:        `<strong>Written thresholds turn 'careful' into a plan.</strong> 'No second country until uptime ≥99.7% and churn <1.5%' is a number the board can hold you to. Now expansion accelerates automatically when the product earns it — and stops when it doesn't.`,
                    coach_r4_soft:          `<strong>'We'll scale prudently' is not a board agreement.</strong> Prudence without thresholds becomes either paralysis or quiet drift. What exact number unlocks the next country? If you can't write it, you haven't decided.`,
                    coach_r4_time:          `<strong>Opening by calendar ignores the product.</strong> A new country every 6 months regardless of uptime or churn is the aggressive plan in disguise — you'll open onto a platform that hasn't earned it and watch the churn climb.`,
                    coach_r4_growthonly:    `<strong>A growth-only dashboard hides the leak.</strong> ARR and new logos look great while churn and uptime quietly rot underneath. The board needs to see the metrics that brake the plan, not only the ones that flatter it.`,
                    coach_r4_nometrics:     `<strong>No fixed dashboard means no governance.</strong> 'Review whenever something comes up' is how the concentration risk and the churn go unseen until they're existential. Name the 4–6 metrics and review them monthly.`,
                    coach_r4_governed:      `<strong>That's a milestone-governed plan both sides can sign.</strong> Strict thresholds, a real governance dashboard and a written brake on churn/runway: growth accelerates when the product earns it and freezes when it doesn't. Reversible risk managed, irreversible risk fenced.`,
                    coach_r4_newround:      `<strong>Relying on a new round is betting on cash you don't have.</strong> The aggressive plan already assumes a next round that isn't guaranteed. 'We'll raise if it goes wrong' is a hope, not a brake — and burns months of runway while you find out.`,
                    coach_r4_push:          `<strong>Accelerating sales to cover a cash gap is the death spiral.</strong> You stress a fragile product with more new customers, churn climbs, and the runway shortens faster. That is exactly how scaling distribution ahead of the product ends the company.`,

